Z||000025||Shadow Wolf||06-05-2000||03:35 AM||||I managed to keep even against a 98 Mustang V6 while driving a Turbo Sprint!  Mind you, anyone who's ever driven a Mustang V6 would realize that you could probably keep up riding a bike.<P>Alas, I don't like to drag my '87 GT though. Once the engine gets above 4000rpm it ticks like crazy.  Though it only cost me $1500 for an otherwise excellent condition car.  Doesn't seem to be short on balls either.||||reg||2
Z||000026||ThatRickGuy||06-05-2000||12:14 PM||||Just a few notes,<BR> someone has to do it, so I'll be the chumpy father figure. BE CARFULE! if at all possible, find a more secluded area, free of curves, if it's a 2 lane road, have some one sit at the end and block traffic. Make sure there aren't people milling arround right next to the line.<P>okay safty lecture done.<BR> As far as pumping up your cars limits, a good old fashioned tune up is the way to go. New plugs/wires/rotor/cap if they are old or worn, oil change, K&N aif filter, check the timing... Note that the spiffy 4 pronged spark plugs (and even just the split fires) don't give you squat for more power, unless your current set is fouled or improperly gapped, in which case replacing them with any spark plug would give you that same increase. Same with the super spark kits, you don't have to worry about a more powerful arch unless you're running very high compression, so for basic road use, you're actually losing power (altho such a tiny ammount you won't feel it) The K&N filters are all that tho. I don't have the article in front of me, but there is a straight up improvement w/ them over standard or no air filter. (They are low resistance and help 'straighten' the air flow) High octane gas will help, it'll let the timing get a little farther forward, and the street chip from the fiero store wouldn't be a bad idea either. Like it was stated above, high preasure in the front, and I'd say play with the back, going a little under recomended would probrably be the good area, to low and the middle of the tire will flex up, you'll lose traction and kill the tires.||||reg||1
Z||000027||batboy||06-05-2000||12:15 PM||||Do not use pure alcohol like was mentioned earlier, jeez! I wouldn't use anymore than about 10% alcohol per volume. Add a little octane booster or mix in some racing fuel maybe. Don't spin the tires, that means you lost traction and aren't going anywhere. Practice a few launches before hand. A short race like that will often be won or lost at the line. One little mess up at the start will cost you the race. Driver skill is important. I'm not so sure of the wisdom of having a crowd of people there though. Seems like the more people that know about a street race, the more likely the police will make an appearance. I'm more in favor of going out in the country to a deserted two lane blacktop for that type of fooling around. Upgrade your wires and coil. Shift at 5 grand. Beat that ricer.||||reg||1
Z||000028||FieroSTETZ||06-06-2000||12:34 AM||||well, it Is in a secluded area- it is kinda like a private qtr mile- a friends dad has a ranch, and just so happens the city started building a road there, and when he proved it was his land they sacraficed it- pretty straight shot one end to the other.<BR>anyone ever heard of felda tires? i dont worry much about spinning because after i heat these up, i can drop the clutch at 4000 and they stil stick- kinda freaky, i was used to the junk tires i had before- right now the drag race is postponed until i get my overheating problem fixed- looks like i might need a head gasket (coolant is dark grey after a week, smells kinda exhausty) i think my head gasket may be leaking compression into my cooling system.||||reg||1
